  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1245 PM MDT Sat Sep 26 2026\n\nPREDOMINATELY DRY THIS WEEKEND:\n\nA flattened high pressure across eastern Utah and Western Colorado \nis bringing mostly sunny skies to the region with subsidence under \nthe ridge and the drier zonal flow aloft. Look for mild Fall \ntemperatures today near normal to five degrees above normal. There \nare some low to mid level clouds developing across the region from \nresidual moisture hanging around and no wind to sweep it out. We may \nsee a few isolated orographic showers and thunderstorms this \nafternoon with the moisture in place, but not seeing any severe \nweather likely. We'll see things start to change Sunday as the \ntrough descends through the Pacific Northwest , shifting the flow \nmore southwesterly pulling the tropical moisture into the region \nfrom hurricanes Odalys and Polo. We'll see increasing clouds moving \ninto the region with the monsoonal surge, but otherwise, it will be \nsimilar to today.  \n\nWET WEEK AHEAD:\n\nThe latest runs of the GFS and GEFS are tracking the low farther \nnorth across southwestern Utah/southern Nevada Monday night which \nwould spell stronger thunderstorms for southeastern Utah and \nsouthwestern Colorado Monday into Tuesday with the cold air \nadvection aloft and the increased dynamics. Pwats are not extreme at \naround an inch, but with the increased instability and dynamics, \ncould produce heavy rain and flash flooding with the stronger \nstorms. The Weather Prediction Center has drawn the southern areas \nunder a moderate risk for excessive rainfall for day three and \nmarginal to slight for day four. There is still a lot of uncertainty \nwith continued inconsistent run-to-run solutions among the models. \nHolding off on issuing headlines 'til we see tonight's or even \ntomorrow's model runs to get another/better look at the system. \nOtherwise, we can look for a cooler, wet  period through Wednesday \nwith showers and thunderstorms, and temperatures five to ten degrees \nbelow normal. Beyond Wednesday, conditions dry out with temperatures \nreturning to near normal for the start of October.",
